mqvpn-server for Debian and Ubuntu
==================================

What the package does
---------------------

On the first install, the package runs /usr/lib/mqvpn/mqvpn-server-setup:

 * If /etc/mqvpn/server.crt and /etc/mqvpn/server.key are both absent, it
   creates a self-signed EC P-256 certificate (365 days). If both exist (for
   example from certbot or your own CA), it keeps them. A symlink counts as
   present, also a symlink to a file that does not exist.
 * If /etc/mqvpn/server.conf is absent, it creates it with a new PSK from
   "mqvpn --genkey". It never changes an existing server.conf.

Then the package enables and starts mqvpn-server.service and prints the
connection summary. If the setup fails, the package does not enable or start
the service: fix the error, then run
"sudo /usr/lib/mqvpn/mqvpn-server-setup --start". To print the summary again,
run:

    sudo mqvpn-server-info

The summary contains the PSK. apt also writes it to /var/log/apt/term.log
(mode 0640, root:adm).

Settings before the install
---------------------------

debconf asks for the public hostname. It asks for the client subnet only
when the default 10.0.0.0/24 overlaps a network of this host, and for the
UDP port only when port 443 is already in use. The other settings take their
defaults. For automation, preseed debconf:

    echo 'mqvpn-server mqvpn-server/subnet string 10.8.0.0/24' | sudo debconf-set-selections
    echo 'mqvpn-server mqvpn-server/hostname string vpn.example.org' | sudo debconf-set-selections
    sudo apt install ./mqvpn_<version>_<arch>.deb ./mqvpn-server_<version>_<arch>.deb

The other debconf keys are mqvpn-server/port, mqvpn-server/enable_control
(boolean), mqvpn-server/control_port and mqvpn-server/autostart (boolean).

Environment variables also work. Default sudoers settings drop variables
from the "sudo VAR=value command" form, so use "sudo env":

    sudo env MQVPN_PORT=10020 MQVPN_SERVER_AUTOSTART=no apt-get install -y \
        ./mqvpn_<version>_<arch>.deb ./mqvpn-server_<version>_<arch>.deb

The variables are MQVPN_PORT, MQVPN_SUBNET, MQVPN_SUBNET6, MQVPN_HOSTNAME,
MQVPN_ENABLE_CONTROL, MQVPN_CONTROL_PORT and MQVPN_SERVER_AUTOSTART.

Precedence, lowest first: built-in defaults, debconf answers,
/etc/default/mqvpn-server, environment variables, mqvpn-server-setup
options.

To use a specific PSK, write /etc/mqvpn/server.conf before the install. The
package never puts the PSK in debconf.

Settings after the install
--------------------------

/etc/mqvpn/server.conf is the authority. Edit it, then run:

    sudo systemctl restart mqvpn-server

To enable the local control API in an existing server.conf, run:

    sudo /usr/lib/mqvpn/mqvpn-server-setup --enable-control [PORT]

The control API has no authentication and no TLS.

Debconf answers and /etc/default/mqvpn-server apply only when
mqvpn-server-setup creates a new server.conf. "dpkg-reconfigure
mqvpn-server" changes the debconf answers, but it does not change an
existing server.conf, and it does not restart the server. To create a new
server.conf from new answers, delete /etc/mqvpn/server.conf (this also
deletes the PSK, so each client needs the new key) and run:

    sudo dpkg-reconfigure mqvpn-server

mqvpn-server-setup alone does not read the debconf answers. It uses
/etc/default/mqvpn-server, the environment and its options.

/etc/default/mqvpn-server and unattended upgrades
-------------------------------------------------

/etc/default/mqvpn-server is a dpkg conffile. All its settings are comments.
If you change it, and a later package version also changes it, dpkg asks
which version to keep. Without a terminal, that question makes the upgrade
fail. Automation must give dpkg a default answer:

    sudo apt-get -o Dpkg::Options::=--force-confdef \
        -o Dpkg::Options::=--force-confold install ./mqvpn-server_<version>_<arch>.deb

With these options, dpkg keeps your file and writes the package version as
/etc/default/mqvpn-server.dpkg-dist.

If you create /etc/default/mqvpn-server before the first install, dpkg also
asks. Use debconf or environment variables before the first install.

Remove and purge
----------------

"apt remove mqvpn-server" stops the server, reverts the NAT rules and the IP
forwarding sysctls, and keeps all files in /etc/mqvpn. If the server was
enabled and running, a later reinstall starts it again.

"apt purge mqvpn-server" also deletes the files that mqvpn-server-setup
created: server.conf, which contains the PSK, and the self-signed certificate
and key. It deletes them also if you edited them. Each client then needs a
new key. Purge never deletes a file that mqvpn-server-setup did not create,
for example a server.conf that you wrote before the install, and never a
symlink, for example a certbot link that replaced the self-signed
certificate. It deletes /etc/mqvpn only when the directory is empty.

Autostart and containers
------------------------

The package enables and starts the service on the first install. To prevent
this, preseed mqvpn-server/autostart to false or set
MQVPN_SERVER_AUTOSTART=no. The package uses deb-systemd-invoke, so a
policy-rc.d that refuses starts (containers, chroots, image builds) also
prevents the start. The autostart answer and a failed setup do not disable a
unit that an earlier mqvpn deb or install.sh enabled; to disable it, run
"systemctl disable mqvpn-server".

On an upgrade, the package restarts the server only if it is running. A
server that you stopped stays stopped.

Migration from install.sh
-------------------------

install.sh puts its files in /usr/local, and the package puts them in /usr.
/usr/local/bin/mqvpn comes first in PATH. Before you install the package,
run the old install.sh with --uninstall. It removes the old files and keeps
/etc/mqvpn. The package then uses your existing server.conf and certificate
without a change, and purge does not delete them.

Do not run "install.sh --uninstall" after you install the package. It
deletes /lib/systemd/system/mqvpn-server.service and
mqvpn-client@.service, and on a merged-/usr system these are the unit files
of the packages. To delete only the install.sh files, run:

    sudo rm -rf /usr/local/bin/mqvpn /usr/local/lib/libmqvpn.so* \
        /usr/local/lib/libxquic.so /usr/local/lib/mqvpn /usr/local/share/doc/mqvpn
    sudo ldconfig
